## AWS EC2 (Elastic Compute Cloud)

    Companies used to buy physical servers. That meant:
        • Huge upfront cost
        • Long procurement cycles
        • Over‑provisioning (buying more than needed)
        • Maintenance, cooling, power
        • Slow scaling
    EC2 solved all of this by offering:
        - No hardware maintenance
        - Scale in seconds
        - Pay only for what you use
        - Global availability

**An EC2 instance is made of 4 core components:**
    1. Compute (CPU + RAM)
    2. Storage (EBS or Instance Store)
    3. Networking (VPC, Subnet, Security Group)
    4. AMI (Operating System template).

**AMI: **
    An AMI (Amazon Machine Image) is like a pre-installed OS snapshot.

    When you launch an EC2 instance, the AMI decides:
        • Which operating system you get (Ubuntu, Amazon Linux, Windows)
        • Whether it includes pre-installed software (e.g., Docker, NGINX, Python)
        • Whether it’s customized for your company

    So the AMI is basically the blueprint for your virtual machine.
    

## What gives an EC2 instance its “power”?
    Every EC2 instance has:
    • vCPUs (virtual CPUs)
    • RAM
    • Network bandwidth
    • Storage performance
    These come from the instance type you choose.


## Instance Type = Hardware Power of Your EC2
    Since you correctly identified that instance type = CPU + RAM configuration, here’s the deeper intuition:
    
    When you choose an instance type like:
    • t2.micro
    • m5.large
    • c6g.xlarge
    
    You are really choosing:
    • how many vCPUs your server gets
    • how much RAM it has
    • what kind of processor (Intel / AMD / ARM)
    • how much network bandwidth
    • how much storage throughput
    
    So the instance type is like choosing the engine of your virtual machine.


# The first letter = the instance family = what the instance is optimized for
    Here are a few examples to make this intuitive:
    • t → burstable, low‑cost general purpose
    • m → balanced general purpose
    • c → compute optimized
    • r → memory optimized
    • i → storage optimized
    • g / p → GPU / accelerated computing.

    So when you see c6g.xlarge, your brain should instantly think:
    “This is a compute‑optimized instance.”


# What does the number represent?
    Example:
        • t2.micro
        • m5.large
        • c6g.xlarge
    AWS uses numbers to indicate generation.
    
    Newer generation =
        • better performance
        • better price/performance
        • newer CPU architecture
        • more efficient networking


    a) The number in the instance type (like 5, 6, 7) = the hardware generation.

    b) Just like phones (iPhone 12 → 13 → 14), EC2 generations improve over time:
        • faster CPUs
        • better network performance
        • lower cost per compute unit
        • newer architecture (Intel → AMD → ARM/Graviton)
    So c6g is newer and more efficient than c5, and m7g is newer than m6g.


   # What does the suffix mean? (like “g”, “i”, “a”)
        Examples:
        • c6g. --> AWS Graviton (ARM-based)
        • m5a  --> AMD EPYC
        • r6i  --> Intel Xeon (x86 architecture)
        • t4g  
        These letters tell you which processor architecture the instance uses.


# What Makes EC2 Instances Actually Run?
    Every EC2 instance needs four things to exist:
    1. AMI → the OS
    2. Instance Type → the hardware
    3. Storage (EBS) → the disk
    4. Networking (VPC + Subnet + Security Group) → connectivity

    EBS = Elastic Block Store = the persistent disk for your EC2 instance
        Think of it like this:
        • Your EC2 instance = the computer
        • Your EBS volume = the SSD / hard disk

        Even if your EC2 instance stops, your EBS volume keeps your data.
        
        This is why EBS is called persistent storage.

# EBS Volume Types
        AWS gives you different types of disks:
            • Some are optimized for speed
            • Some for IOPS
            • Some for throughput
            • Some for low cost

# EBS is not physically inside the EC2 instance.
        It’s more like a network‑attached SSD.
        This means:
        • your EC2 instance accesses the disk over the network
        • the disk lives independently of the instance
        • you can detach it
        • you can attach it to another instance
        • it persists even if the instance stops
        This is why EBS is so flexible.
